.TH 2089 6 "October 2026" "2089 0.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
2089 \- the game of 2048, for terminals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 2089
[
.B \-t
] [
.B \-k
|
.B \-K
] [
.B \-m
|
.B \-p
] [
.B \-a
] [
.B \-u
] [
.B \-s
.I size
] [
.B \-r
.I seed
] [
.B \-l
.I savestring
]
.br
.B 2089
.B \-v
|
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B 2089
is the game of 2048, after Gabriele Cirulli's.
Each move slides every tile on the board as far as it will go, up,
down, left or right.
Two tiles of the same number that meet join into one worth both, and
the score goes up by its value.
After every move a new tile, a 2 or now and then a 4, appears in an
empty cell.
Reach 2048 to win; then keep going if you like, until no move is left.
.PP
.B 2089
plays full screen, through curses, when the terminal can.
It falls back to line mode by itself when the input or output is not a
terminal, when
.B TERM
is unknown or names a hardcopy terminal, when the screen is too small
for the board, or when asked with
.BR \-t .
A
.B 2089
built without curses plays in line mode only.
.SS Full screen
Every key is a move, as in the original game.
The board is drawn in one of three styles, the best the terminal can
show, and
.B c
switches between them:
.TP
.B color
tiles on colored backgrounds, with 256, 16 or 8 colors;
.TP
.B mono
tiles in bold and reverse video;
.TP
.B plain
numbers only, with no video attributes, for terminals whose attributes
take up a place on the screen (the Wyse 50, for one).
These get plain without asking.
.PP
Cells are three lines high where there's room, and one line high on a
line of 4800 baud or less in color or mono, which sends about a third
as much on each move.
Box lines use the terminal's line-drawing characters where it has them.
.SS Line mode
One line is one move: type a key, then Return.
If a line holds several keys, the last one counts.
.B n
and
.B q
count only on a line of their own, so a slip can't end a game, and
Return alone draws the board again.
Arrow keys work here too.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
Line mode, even on a terminal that could play full screen.
.TP
.B \-k
Compact: cells one line high full screen; in line mode, the compact
display (one line of numbers for each row), for slow lines and paper.
.TP
.B \-K
Not compact: tall cells even on a slow line, and the boxed display in
line mode.
.TP
.B \-m
Mono: no color, tiles in bold and reverse video.
.TP
.B \-p
Plain: no video attributes at all.
.TP
.B \-a
Draw boxes with
.BR + ,
.B \-
and
.B |
instead of line-drawing characters.
.TP
.B \-u
Allow undo.
Undo is off unless asked for: living with a bad move is part of the
game.
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
A board
.I size
cells across, from 2 to 8.
The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-r " seed"
Play game number
.IR seed ,
from 0 to 4294967295.
The same number and size always deal the same game, in any 2089
program.
.TP
.BI \-l " savestring"
Carry on a saved game.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a summary of the options.
.PP
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-m ,
.B \-p
and
.B \-a
are only in a
.B 2089
built with curses.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR "w a s d" , " k h j l" , " 8 4 2 6" ", or the arrow keys"
Move up, left, down or right.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last move, if undo is on (\c
.BR \-u ).
.TP
.B n
Start a new game: full screen after asking, in line mode on a line of
its own.
.TP
.B p
Show the save string of the game being played.
.TP
.B c
Full screen, the next display style; in line mode, the compact display
on or off.
.TP
.B ?
The keys.
.TP
.B ^L
Draw the screen again (full screen).
.TP
.B q
Quit, printing the command that carries on the game.
.SH SAVE STRINGS
A game can be written as a short string of digits, small letters and
dots, safe to copy anywhere: into a mail message, a file name or a URL.
.B p
shows it,
.B \-l
plays it, and on quitting
.B 2089
prints the command that carries on, such as
.PP
.RS
.nf
to carry on later: 2089 \-u \-l 14b1000100000000000.0.0.3a10575b
.fi
.RE
.PP
The strings are the same in
.B x2089
and
.BR xm2089 .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.2089
The best score for each board size, kept full screen and by the X
programs: one save string a line, the game as it stood at its best, so
that
.B 2089 \-l
brings it back.
A
.B 2089
built with
.B T89_SCORES
defined keeps them in the file it names instead.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
The terminal, for curses.
.TP
.B HOME
Where the best scores are kept.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the game is quit; more than 0 after a bad option or save string,
or when stopped by a signal (the command that carries on is printed
even then).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR x2089 (6),
.BR xm2089 (6)
.PP
